Here is an example of how to generate a table of contents (TOC) for a LATEX document using the `toc` package:

```
\documentclass{article}
\usepackage[utf8]{inputenc}
\usepackage[T1]{fontenc}
\usepackage{lmodern}
\usepackage{amsmath,amsfonts,amssymb}
\usepackage{graphicx}

% Define the TOC command
\newcommand{\toc}[2]{
  \setcounter{secnumdepth}{0} % Set the depth of section numbers to zero
  \renewcommand*{\contentsname}{Table of Contents}
  \tableofcontents
}

% Example usage
\begin{document}
\maketitle

\section{Introduction}
\label{sec:intro}

\subsection{Background}
\label{sub:background}

\subsection{Motivation}
\label{sub:motivation}

\section{Methodology}
\label{sec:methodology}

\subsection{Data Collection}
\label{sub:data-collection}

\subsection{Data Analysis}
\label{sub:data-analysis}

\subsection{Results}
\label{sub:results}

\subsection{Discussion}
\label{sub:discussion}

\subsection{Conclusion}
\label{sub:conclusion}

\end{document}
```

In this example, we first define the `\toc` command that sets the depth of section numbers to zero and creates a table of contents with the title "Table of Contents". We then use the `\setcounter` and `\renewcommand` commands to set the depth of section numbers to one.

Next, we include three sections within the TOC, each containing sub-sections under their respective headings. Each section has its own heading (`\label`) and uses the `\subsection` command to create sub-sections within it.

Finally, we add a footer section with the titles of each section.

When you run this code, you should see a table of contents with the following structure:

```
\title{Your Document Title}
\author{}
\date{}

\maketitle

\tableofcontents

\listoffigures
\listoftables

\backmatter
\printindex
``` 

This will produce a table of contents with the desired layout and formatting. You can customize the appearance of the TOC by modifying the `\setcounter` and `\renewcommand` commands as needed.

