The == operator is used for primitive data types like int, long, byte, short, char, etc., while the equals() method is used for objects (classes) in Java. The main difference between the two operators is that == compares the values of variables, whereas equals() checks if two objects are equal by comparing their contents.

When you use == to compare strings in Java, it will only work if they have the same length and content. If the strings differ in size or content, then they won't be considered equal. For example:

```java
String str1 = "Hello";
String str2 = "Hello";
System.out.println(str1.equals(str2)); // false

str1 = "World";
str2 = "Hello";
System.out.println(str1.equals(str2)); // true
```

In this case, str1 and str2 are different strings because they have different lengths and contain different characters.

On the other hand, when you use equals(), you can check if two objects are actually the same object, even if they have different content. This is useful when you want to ensure that two objects refer to the same memory location:

```java
Object obj1 = new Object();
Object obj2 = new Object();

if (obj1.equals(obj2)) {
    System.out.println("Objects are equal");
} else {
    System.out.println("Objects are not equal");
}
```

In this case, obj1 and obj2 are both instances of the Object class, but they may not be pointing to the same memory location. Therefore, calling equals() on them would return false.

Overall, it's recommended to stick with == for primitive data types like int, long, byte, short, char, and boolean. But if you need to compare objects, always use equals().

